#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529220 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{vmatrix}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5})+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)(- \\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{3}{10})+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}(\\frac{12 \\sqrt{2}}{25}- \\frac{6 \\sqrt{2}}{25}) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot \\frac{6 \\sqrt{2}}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}+\\frac{3}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=\\frac{6}{25}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$D$ is **not** a singular matrix” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529229 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & 1 & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & 1 & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{8})+(-1)(- \\frac{7}{5}+\\frac{21}{20})+\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{10}+\\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{10}) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{8}+(-1)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{7}{20}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{7}{40}+\\frac{7}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=\\frac{7}{40}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$D$ is **not** a singular matrix” and $det(D)=\\frac{7}{40}$ are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529250 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nN=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 1 & 2 \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -2 & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 1 & 2 \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -2 & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{1}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -2 & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+2 \\sqrt{2}\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}+2 \\sqrt{2})+(-1)(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{8}{3})+2 \\sqrt{2}(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{10 \\sqrt{2}}{3}+(-1)\\cdot \\frac{16}{3}+2 \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{9}- \\frac{16}{3}+\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$N$ is a singular matrix” and $det(N)=0$ are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529336 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\left({u}^{2}+1\\right),\\,y=4\\left({v}^{3}+1\\right),\\,z=2\\left(2u+v\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨3\\left({u}^{2}+1\\right),\\,4\\left({v}^{3}+1\\right),\\,2\\left(2u+v\\right)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3\\left({u}^{2}+1\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4\\left({v}^{3}+1\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2\\left(2u+v\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6 u,\\,0,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3\\left({u}^{2}\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4\\left({v}^{3}+1\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2\\left(2u+v\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,12 v^{2},\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3\\left(2u\\right) & 0 & 2\\left(2\\right) \\\\ 0 & 4\\left(3{v}^{2}\\right) & 2\\left(1\\right)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2\\left(2\\right) \\\\ 4\\left(3{v}^{2}\\right) & 2\\left(1\\right)\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}3\\left(2u\\right) & 2\\left(2\\right) \\\\ 0 & 2\\left(1\\right)\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}3\\left(2u\\right) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\left(3{v}^{2}\\right)\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 48 v^{2},\\,- 12 u,\\,72 u v^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529341 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+1,\\,y=3\\left({v}^{3}+1\\right),\\,z=4\\left(2u+v\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2}+1,\\,3\\left({v}^{3}+1\\right),\\,4\\left(2u+v\\right)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}+1),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3\\left({v}^{3}+1\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4\\left(2u+v\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,0,\\,8⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3\\left({v}^{3}+1\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4\\left(2u+v\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,9 v^{2},\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 0 & 4\\left(2\\right) \\\\ 0 & 3\\left(3{v}^{2}\\right) & 4\\left(1\\right)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4\\left(2\\right) \\\\ 3\\left(3{v}^{2}\\right) & 4\\left(1\\right)\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 4\\left(2\\right) \\\\ 0 & 4\\left(1\\right)\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\left(3{v}^{2}\\right)\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 72 v^{2},\\,- 8 u,\\,18 u v^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529345 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = 2\\left(u + v\\right), y = 3\\left(v^{2} + \\sin u\\right), z = uv.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2 u + 2 v,\\,3 v^{2} + 3 \\sin{(u )},\\,u v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 u + 2 v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3 v^{2} + 3 \\sin{(u )}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,3 \\cos{(u )},\\,v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 u + 2 v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3 v^{2} + 3 \\sin{(u )}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,6 v,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 \\cos{(u )} & v \\\\ 2 & 6 v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\cos{(u )} & v \\\\ 6 v & u\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & v \\\\ 2 & u\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\cos{(u )} \\\\ 2 & 6 v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(3 \\cos{(u )})(u)-(v)(6 v),\\,-[(2)(u)-(v)(2)],\\,(2)(6 v)-(3 \\cos{(u )})(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3 u \\cos{(u )} - 6 v^{2},\\,- 2 u + 2 v,\\,12 v - 6 \\cos{(u )}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529348 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\left({u}^{2}+1\\right),\\,y=4\\left({v}^{3}+1\\right),\\,z=2\\left(2u+v\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨4\\left({u}^{2}+1\\right),\\,4\\left({v}^{3}+1\\right),\\,2\\left(2u+v\\right)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4\\left({u}^{2}+1\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4\\left({v}^{3}+1\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2\\left(2u+v\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8 u,\\,0,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4\\left({u}^{2}\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4\\left({v}^{3}+1\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2\\left(2u+v\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,12 v^{2},\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4\\left(2u\\right) & 0 & 2\\left(2\\right) \\\\ 0 & 4\\left(3{v}^{2}\\right) & 2\\left(1\\right)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2\\left(2\\right) \\\\ 4\\left(3{v}^{2}\\right) & 2\\left(1\\right)\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}4\\left(2u\\right) & 2\\left(2\\right) \\\\ 0 & 2\\left(1\\right)\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}4\\left(2u\\right) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\left(3{v}^{2}\\right)\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 48 v^{2},\\,- 16 u,\\,96 u v^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529356 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\left(u\\right),\\,y=3\\left(v\\right),\\,z=4\\left(u-{e}^{v}\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2\\left(u\\right),\\,3\\left(v\\right),\\,4\\left(u-{e}^{v}\\right)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2\\left(u\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3\\left(v\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4\\left(u-{e}^{v}\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,0,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2\\left(u\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3\\left(v\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4\\left(u-{e}^{v}\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,3,\\,- 4 e^{v}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2\\left(1\\right) & 0 & 4\\left(1\\right) \\\\ 0 & 3\\left(1\\right) & 4\\left(-{e}^{v}\\right)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4\\left(1\\right) \\\\ 3\\left(1\\right) & 4\\left(-{e}^{v}\\right)\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2\\left(1\\right) & 4\\left(1\\right) \\\\ 0 & 4\\left(-{e}^{v}\\right)\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2\\left(1\\right) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\left(1\\right)\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-12,\\,8 e^{v},\\,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529359 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface generated by the vector-valued function $r(u,v)=⟨3\\left(u\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3\\left(u\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,-v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3\\left(u\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3\\left(1\\right) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡v & -v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ 0 & u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & -v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}3\\left(1\\right) & -v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}3\\left(1\\right) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ 0 & u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v+uv\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,- 3 \\cos{(u )},\\,3 u \\cos{(v )}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529380 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = 3\\left(u + v\\right), y = 3\\left(v^{2} + \\sin u\\right), z = 3\\left(uv\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨3 u + 3 v,\\,3 v^{2} + 3 \\sin{(u )},\\,3 u v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3 u + 3 v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3 v^{2} + 3 \\sin{(u )}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3 u v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,3 \\cos{(u )},\\,3 v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3 u + 3 v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3 v^{2} + 3 \\sin{(u )}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3 u v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,6 v,\\,3 u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 3 \\cos{(u )} & 3 v \\\\ 3 & 6 v & 3 u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\cos{(u )} & 3 v \\\\ 6 v & 3 u\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3 v \\\\ 3 & 3 u\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3 \\cos{(u )} \\\\ 3 & 6 v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(3 \\cos{(u )})(3 u)-(3 v)(6 v),\\,-[(3)(3 u)-(3 v)(3)],\\,(3)(6 v)-(3 \\cos{(u )})(3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9 u \\cos{(u )} - 18 v^{2},\\,- 9 u + 9 v,\\,18 v - 9 \\cos{(u )}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529385 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\left({u}^{2}+1\\right),\\,y=3\\left({v}^{3}+1\\right),\\,z=4\\left(2u+v\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2\\left({u}^{2}+1\\right),\\,3\\left({v}^{3}+1\\right),\\,4\\left(2u+v\\right)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2\\left({u}^{2}+1\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3\\left({v}^{3}+1\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4\\left(2u+v\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4 u,\\,0,\\,8⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2\\left({u}^{2}\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3\\left({v}^{3}+1\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4\\left(2u+v\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,9 v^{2},\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2\\left(2u\\right) & 0 & 4\\left(2\\right) \\\\ 0 & 3\\left(3{v}^{2}\\right) & 4\\left(1\\right)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4\\left(2\\right) \\\\ 3\\left(3{v}^{2}\\right) & 4\\left(1\\right)\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2\\left(2u\\right) & 4\\left(2\\right) \\\\ 0 & 4\\left(1\\right)\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2\\left(2u\\right) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\left(3{v}^{2}\\right)\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 72 v^{2},\\,- 16 u,\\,36 u v^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529391 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx=u,\\,y=3\\left(v\\right),\\,z=4\\left(u-{e}^{v}\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,3\\left(v\\right),\\,4\\left(u-{e}^{v}\\right)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3\\left(v\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4\\left(u-{e}^{v}\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3\\left(v\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4\\left(u-{e}^{v}\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,3,\\,- 4 e^{v}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\left(1\\right) \\\\ 0 & 3\\left(1\\right) & 4\\left(-{e}^{v}\\right)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4\\left(1\\right) \\\\ 3\\left(1\\right) & 4\\left(-{e}^{v}\\right)\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4\\left(1\\right) \\\\ 0 & 4\\left(-{e}^{v}\\right)\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\left(1\\right)\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-12,\\,4 e^{v},\\,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529402 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\left({u}^{2}+1\\right),\\,y={v}^{3}+1,\\,z=2\\left(2u+v\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨3\\left({u}^{2}+1\\right),\\,{v}^{3}+1,\\,2\\left(2u+v\\right)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3\\left({u}^{2}+1\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{3}+1),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2\\left(2u+v\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6 u,\\,0,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3\\left({u}^{2}\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{3}+1),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2\\left(2u+v\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,3{v}^{2},\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3\\left(2u\\right) & 0 & 2\\left(2\\right) \\\\ 0 & 3{v}^{2} & 2\\left(1\\right)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2\\left(2\\right) \\\\ 3{v}^{2} & 2\\left(1\\right)\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}3\\left(2u\\right) & 2\\left(2\\right) \\\\ 0 & 2\\left(1\\right)\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}3\\left(2u\\right) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3{v}^{2}\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 12 v^{2},\\,- 12 u,\\,18 u v^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529409 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\left({u}^{2}+1\\right),\\,y=2\\left({v}^{3}+1\\right),\\,z=2u+v.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨4\\left({u}^{2}+1\\right),\\,2\\left({v}^{3}+1\\right),\\,2u+v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4\\left({u}^{2}+1\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2\\left({v}^{3}+1\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8 u,\\,0,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4\\left({u}^{2}\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2\\left({v}^{3}+1\\right)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,6 v^{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4\\left(2u\\right) & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\left(3{v}^{2}\\right) & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2\\left(3{v}^{2}\\right) & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}4\\left(2u\\right) & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}4\\left(2u\\right) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\left(3{v}^{2}\\right)\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 12 v^{2},\\,- 8 u,\\,48 u v^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529474 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=- 3 x - 3 y,$ where $x=- s - 2 t$ and $y=3 s + 3 t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=-3,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-3, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-2, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot (3) \\\\  & =(6)+(-9) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529477 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=3 x + y,$ where $x=- 2 s - 3 t$ and $y=s - 3 t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=3,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =3, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-3, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(3)\\cdot (-3)+(1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =(-9)+(-3) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529480 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=2 x - 3 y,$ where $x=s - t$ and $y=2 s - 2 t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=2,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(2)\\cdot (1)+(-3)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =(2)+(-6) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529488 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=- 2 x - 3 y,$ where $x=3 s - t$ and $y=- s - 2 t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=-2,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-2, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =3, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (3)+(-3)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =(-6)+(3) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529494 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x - 3 y,$ where $x=- s + 2 t$ and $y=s + t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =2, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(1)\\cdot (2)+(-3)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =(2)+(-3) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529511 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $-2\\sin^{5}{\\alpha}-2\\sin^{3}{\\alpha}\\cos^{2}{\\alpha}-3\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}\\cos^{3}{\\alpha}-3\\cos^{5}{\\alpha}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n-2\\sin^{5}{\\alpha}-2\\sin^{3}{\\alpha}\\cos^{2}{\\alpha}-3\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}\\cos^{3}{\\alpha}-3\\cos^{5}{\\alpha}=-2\\sin^{3}{\\alpha}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}+\\cos^{2}{\\alpha})-3\\cos^{3}{\\alpha}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}+\\cos^{2}{\\alpha})\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}+\\cos^{2}{\\alpha}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\sin^{3}{\\alpha}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}+\\cos^{2}{\\alpha})-3\\cos^{3}{\\alpha}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}+\\cos^{2}{\\alpha}) & =-2\\sin^{3}{\\alpha}\\cdot 1-3\\cos^{3}{\\alpha}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\sin^{3}{\\alpha}-3\\cos^{3}{\\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-2\\sin^{5}{\\alpha}-2\\sin^{3}{\\alpha}\\cos^{2}{\\alpha}-3\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}\\cos^{3}{\\alpha}-3\\cos^{5}{\\alpha}=-2\\sin^{3}{\\alpha}-3\\cos^{3}{\\alpha}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529517 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $-4\\sin^{4}{t}\\cos{t}-4\\sin^{2}{t}\\cos^{3}{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n-4\\sin^{4}{t}\\cos{t}-4\\sin^{2}{t}\\cos^{3}{t}=-4\\sin^{2}{t}\\cos{t}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t})\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\sin^{2}{t}\\cos{t}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}) & =-4\\sin^{2}{t}\\cos{t}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4\\sin^{2}{t}\\cos{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-4\\sin^{4}{t}\\cos{t}-4\\sin^{2}{t}\\cos^{3}{t}=-4\\sin^{2}{t}\\cos{t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529524 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $-2\\sin^{3}{t}-2\\sin{t}\\cos^{2}{t}-4\\sin^{2}{t}\\cos{t}-4\\cos^{3}{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n-2\\sin^{3}{t}-2\\sin{t}\\cos^{2}{t}-4\\sin^{2}{t}\\cos{t}-4\\cos^{3}{t}=-2\\sin{t}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t})-4\\cos{t}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t})\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\sin{t}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t})-4\\cos{t}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}) & =-2\\sin{t}\\cdot 1-4\\cos{t}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\sin{t}-4\\cos{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-2\\sin^{3}{t}-2\\sin{t}\\cos^{2}{t}-4\\sin^{2}{t}\\cos{t}-4\\cos^{3}{t}=-2\\sin{t}-4\\cos{t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529544 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $4\\sin^{5}{\\theta}+4\\sin^{3}{\\theta}\\cos^{2}{\\theta}-4\\sin^{2}{\\theta}\\cos^{3}{\\theta}-4\\cos^{5}{\\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n4\\sin^{5}{\\theta}+4\\sin^{3}{\\theta}\\cos^{2}{\\theta}-4\\sin^{2}{\\theta}\\cos^{3}{\\theta}-4\\cos^{5}{\\theta}=4\\sin^{3}{\\theta}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{\\theta}+\\cos^{2}{\\theta})-4\\cos^{3}{\\theta}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{\\theta}+\\cos^{2}{\\theta})\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^{2}{\\theta}+\\cos^{2}{\\theta}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sin^{3}{\\theta}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{\\theta}+\\cos^{2}{\\theta})-4\\cos^{3}{\\theta}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{\\theta}+\\cos^{2}{\\theta}) & =4\\sin^{3}{\\theta}\\cdot 1-4\\cos^{3}{\\theta}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\sin^{3}{\\theta}-4\\cos^{3}{\\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n4\\sin^{5}{\\theta}+4\\sin^{3}{\\theta}\\cos^{2}{\\theta}-4\\sin^{2}{\\theta}\\cos^{3}{\\theta}-4\\cos^{5}{\\theta}=4\\sin^{3}{\\theta}-4\\cos^{3}{\\theta}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529545 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $-5\\sin^{4}{\\alpha}-5\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}\\cos^{2}{\\alpha}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n-5\\sin^{4}{\\alpha}-5\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}\\cos^{2}{\\alpha}=-5\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}+\\cos^{2}{\\alpha})\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}+\\cos^{2}{\\alpha}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}+\\cos^{2}{\\alpha}) & =-5\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-5\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-5\\sin^{4}{\\alpha}-5\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}\\cos^{2}{\\alpha}=-5\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529551 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $-2\\sin^{3}{\\alpha}-2\\sin{\\alpha}\\cos^{2}{\\alpha}+3\\sin{\\alpha}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n-2\\sin^{3}{\\alpha}-2\\sin{\\alpha}\\cos^{2}{\\alpha}+3\\sin{\\alpha}=-2\\sin{\\alpha}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}+\\cos^{2}{\\alpha})+3\\sin{\\alpha}\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}+\\cos^{2}{\\alpha}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\sin{\\alpha}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}+\\cos^{2}{\\alpha})+3\\sin{\\alpha} & =-2\\sin{\\alpha}\\cdot 1+3\\sin{\\alpha} \\\\  & =-2\\sin{\\alpha}+3\\sin{\\alpha} \\\\  & =\\sin{\\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-2\\sin^{3}{\\alpha}-2\\sin{\\alpha}\\cos^{2}{\\alpha}+3\\sin{\\alpha}=\\sin{\\alpha}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529552 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $-3\\sin^{5}{t}-3\\sin^{3}{t}\\cos^{2}{t}+4\\sin^{2}{t}\\cos{t}+4\\cos^{3}{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n-3\\sin^{5}{t}-3\\sin^{3}{t}\\cos^{2}{t}+4\\sin^{2}{t}\\cos{t}+4\\cos^{3}{t}=-3\\sin^{3}{t}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t})+4\\cos{t}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t})\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\sin^{3}{t}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t})+4\\cos{t}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}) & =-3\\sin^{3}{t}\\cdot 1+4\\cos{t}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-3\\sin^{3}{t}+4\\cos{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-3\\sin^{5}{t}-3\\sin^{3}{t}\\cos^{2}{t}+4\\sin^{2}{t}\\cos{t}+4\\cos^{3}{t}=-3\\sin^{3}{t}+4\\cos{t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529559 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $4\\sin^{3}{x}\\cos^{2}{x}+4\\sin{x}\\cos^{4}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n4\\sin^{3}{x}\\cos^{2}{x}+4\\sin{x}\\cos^{4}{x}=4\\sin{x}\\cos^{2}{x}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{x}+\\cos^{2}{x})\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^{2}{x}+\\cos^{2}{x}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sin{x}\\cos^{2}{x}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{x}+\\cos^{2}{x}) & =4\\sin{x}\\cos^{2}{x}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\sin{x}\\cos^{2}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n4\\sin^{3}{x}\\cos^{2}{x}+4\\sin{x}\\cos^{4}{x}=4\\sin{x}\\cos^{2}{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529563 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $-3\\sin^{5}{t}-3\\sin^{3}{t}\\cos^{2}{t}-\\sin^{2}{t}\\cos^{3}{t}-\\cos^{5}{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n-3\\sin^{5}{t}-3\\sin^{3}{t}\\cos^{2}{t}-\\sin^{2}{t}\\cos^{3}{t}-\\cos^{5}{t}=-3\\sin^{3}{t}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t})-\\cos^{3}{t}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t})\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\sin^{3}{t}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t})-\\cos^{3}{t}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}) & =-3\\sin^{3}{t}\\cdot 1-\\cos^{3}{t}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-3\\sin^{3}{t}-\\cos^{3}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-3\\sin^{5}{t}-3\\sin^{3}{t}\\cos^{2}{t}-\\sin^{2}{t}\\cos^{3}{t}-\\cos^{5}{t}=-3\\sin^{3}{t}-\\cos^{3}{t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530432 :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $c=0$ gives two lines\n\nII. $c=1$ gives a circle\n\nIII. $c=2$ gives a hyperbola" :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0$ the level curves are $y=\\pm 2x,$ which consists of two lines.\n\n- If $c=1$ the level curve is $4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=1,$ which is a hyperbola.\n\n- If $c=2$ the level curve is $2{x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1,$ which is a hyperbola.\n\nSo, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530438 :question/problem "![](images/63/634a863f2154398762af17da40b22d303b10b8846c90fa2021148779e1d1fa97.png)\n\nWhich of the following could represent the level curves of the surface $z=2{x}^{2}$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[0,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\ge 0.$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =c \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{c}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in the equations $x=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{c}{2}}$ do not depend on $y.$ So, for a given $c\\ge 0$, the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of vertical lines, as shown below.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/84/844b92278c8b945a68104629b8dcb14d1b41c6875677ffda4da9615363e55221.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530581 :question/problem "Differentiate $(4 x^{4} + 1)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=4 x^{4} + 1$ and $v(x)=\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((4 x^{4} + 1)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}))^{'} & =u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ & =(4 x^{4} + 1)'\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)})+(4 x^{4} + 1)\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)})' \\\\ & =(16 x^{3})(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)})+(4 x^{4} + 1)(\\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1) \\\\ & =4 x^{4} \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 4 x^{4} + 16 x^{3} \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530582 :question/problem "Differentiate $(4 x^{3} + 4)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=4 x^{3} + 4$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((4 x^{3} + 4)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}))^{'} & =u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ & =(4 x^{3} + 4)'\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})+(4 x^{3} + 4)\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})' \\\\ & =(12 x^{2})(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})+(4 x^{3} + 4)(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ & =4 x^{3} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 12 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530585 :question/problem "Differentiate $(4 x^{4} + x)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 2).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=4 x^{4} + x$ and $v(x)=\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 2.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((4 x^{4} + x)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 2))^{'} & =u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ & =(4 x^{4} + x)'\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 2)+(4 x^{4} + x)\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 2)' \\\\ & =(16 x^{3} + 1)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 2)+(4 x^{4} + x)(\\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1) \\\\ & =4 x^{4} \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 4 x^{4} + 16 x^{3} \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 32 x^{3} + x \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + x + \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530605 :question/problem "Differentiate $(4 - x^{2})(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=4 - x^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((4 - x^{2})(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}))^{'} & =u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ & =(4 - x^{2})'\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)})+(4 - x^{2})\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)})' \\\\ & =(- 2 x)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)})+(4 - x^{2})(\\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1) \\\\ & =- x^{2} \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} - x^{2} - 2 x \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 4 \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530609 :question/problem "Differentiate $(- x^{2} + 4 x)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} - 3).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=- x^{2} + 4 x$ and $v(x)=\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} - 3.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((- x^{2} + 4 x)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} - 3))^{'} & =u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ & =(- x^{2} + 4 x)'\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} - 3)+(- x^{2} + 4 x)\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} - 3)' \\\\ & =(4 - 2 x)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} - 3)+(- x^{2} + 4 x)(\\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1) \\\\ & =- x^{2} \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} - x^{2} + 4 x \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 x \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 10 x + 4 \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} - 12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530616 :question/problem "Differentiate $(4 x^{3} + 4 x)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 4).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=4 x^{3} + 4 x$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 4.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((4 x^{3} + 4 x)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 4))^{'} & =u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ & =(4 x^{3} + 4 x)'\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 4)+(4 x^{3} + 4 x)\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 4)' \\\\ & =(12 x^{2} + 4)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 4)+(4 x^{3} + 4 x)(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ & =4 x^{3} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 12 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 48 x^{2} + 4 x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 4 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 16\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530624 :question/problem "Differentiate $(3 x^{2} - 2 x)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 1).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=3 x^{2} - 2 x$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 1.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((3 x^{2} - 2 x)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 1))^{'} & =u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ & =(3 x^{2} - 2 x)'\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 1)+(3 x^{2} - 2 x)\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 1)' \\\\ & =(6 x - 2)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 1)+(3 x^{2} - 2 x)(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ & =3 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 6 x \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - 6 x - 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530627 :question/problem "Differentiate $(- 2 x^{2} - 2 x)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 4).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=- 2 x^{2} - 2 x$ and $v(x)=\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 4.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((- 2 x^{2} - 2 x)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 4))^{'} & =u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ & =(- 2 x^{2} - 2 x)'\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 4)+(- 2 x^{2} - 2 x)\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 4)' \\\\ & =(- 4 x - 2)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 4)+(- 2 x^{2} - 2 x)(\\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1) \\\\ & =- 2 x^{2} \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 x^{2} - 2 x \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} - 4 x \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} - 18 x - 2 \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} - 8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530628 :question/problem "Differentiate $(3 x^{2} - 2)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=3 x^{2} - 2$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((3 x^{2} - 2)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}))^{'} & =u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ & =(3 x^{2} - 2)'\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})+(3 x^{2} - 2)\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})' \\\\ & =(6 x)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})+(3 x^{2} - 2)(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ & =3 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 6 x \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530641 :question/problem "Differentiate $(- 2 x^{2} - 2)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=- 2 x^{2} - 2$ and $v(x)=\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((- 2 x^{2} - 2)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}))^{'} & =u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ & =(- 2 x^{2} - 2)'\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)})+(- 2 x^{2} - 2)\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)})' \\\\ & =(- 4 x)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)})+(- 2 x^{2} - 2)(\\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1) \\\\ & =- 2 x^{2} \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 x^{2} - 4 x \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
